---
// "Two doors in" — who the course is for, in the reader's own situations.
// Door one is class two (someone hurt you), door two is class three (you did
// something, or didn't). The examples come from Jacob's and Daniela's notes on
// the people who come (November 2026); keep them concrete and plain, in the
// second person, and never more graphic than these.
const doorOne = [
  'Your sister stopped speaking to you after your mother’s funeral.',
  'Your father left when you were nine and never called.',
  'Your mother taught you that good girls never raise their voices, and you kept quiet through thirty years of family dinners.',
  'Your grown daughter cut off contact and won’t say why.',
  'A group of girls bullied you for years, and the teacher looked away.',
  'Someone you trusted hurt you, and when you told, no one believed you.',
  'Your husband had an affair, and then told your friends a different story.',
  'You were let go after 22 years, the week you came back from caring for a parent.',
  'A doctor dismissed your symptoms for years.',
  'Your brother died by suicide. You keep asking why, and there is an anger you hide.',
  'Your congregation turned away from you after your divorce.',
];

const doorTwo = [
  'You fell asleep while watching your father, and he got up, fell and hurt himself.',
  'You weren’t in the room when your mother died.',
  'The last words you said to a friend were an argument, and she died the next week.',
  'You agreed to take your husband off life support.',
  'You were short-tempered through your son’s teenage years, and he moved out at eighteen.',
  'You worked long hours and missed your children’s childhood.',
  'You didn’t believe your daughter when she told you what happened.',
  'You left the gate open, and your dog ran into the road.',
  'As a child you bullied a girl in your class, and you have never forgotten her face.',
  'You glanced at your phone, and the other driver went to the hospital.',
];
---

<section class="fc-doors">
  <div class="fc-doors-inner">
    <div class="fc-doors-head">
      <div class="fc-eyebrow">Two doors in</div>
      <h2 class="fc-h2">Someone hurt you. Or you hurt someone. <em>Often both.</em></h2>
      <p class="fc-lead">
        It usually starts with a wound that has a name: rejection, abandonment,
        betrayal, humiliation, injustice. Sometimes it is a life you feel trapped
        in, or needs that were never met.
      </p>
      <p class="fc-lead">
        And then you find a way to live with it. You stay quiet and polite, and
        the resentment builds. You keep your distance from the people or the
        place that hurt you. You stay busy, so there is no time to feel it. You
        replay the conversation you never had.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="fc-doors-grid">
      <article class="fc-door">
        <div class="fc-door-label">Door one <span>· Class two</span></div>
        <h3 class="fc-door-title">Someone or something hurt you</h3>
        <p class="fc-door-sub">
          A person, a family, a workplace, a doctor, a church, a culture. Or
          someone you love was hurt.
        </p>
        <ul class="fc-door-list">{doorOne.map((t) => <li>{t}</li>)}</ul>
      </article>
      <article class="fc-door">
        <div class="fc-door-label">Door two <span>· Class three</span></div>
        <h3 class="fc-door-title">You did something, or didn’t</h3>
        <p class="fc-door-sub">
          And you keep living it again. <em>I’m not good enough. I’m unforgivable.
          I’m a bad person.</em>
        </p>
        <ul class="fc-door-list">{doorTwo.map((t) => <li>{t}</li>)}</ul>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p class="fc-doors-foot">
      Whichever door you come through, class one begins with why
      <em>“just let it go”</em> never worked, and class four brings it all home,
      into the days you actually live.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
